<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#indus-script">Glossary</a> · Term · script</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indus script</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2800–1900 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/29th-century-bce">29th century BCE</a> · Also known as Harappan script, Indus Valley script</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> script, also called the Harappan script, is the set of signs used by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a>, found chiefly on stamp seals, seal impressions and pottery. Early examples date from about 2800–2600 BCE, and by 1992 some 4,000 inscribed objects bearing over 400 distinct signs were known, generally read from right to left. Because the inscriptions are very short, averaging about five signs, and no bilingual text has been found, it remains undeciphered, and scholars disagree whether it records a language at all.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/indus-script.jpg" alt="Indus script" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: World Imaging · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IndusValleySeals.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Early examples of the script come from pottery inscriptions and clay impressions of inscribed seals dated to about 2800–2600 BCE, in the Early Harappan period, where they appear alongside administrative objects such as seals and standardised weights. Excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harappa">Harappa</a> show that some symbols developed from potters' marks and graffiti of the earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravi">Ravi</a> phase, about 3500–2800 BCE. In the Mature Harappan period, from about 2600 to 1900 BCE, strings of signs are common on flat rectangular stamp seals and on pottery, tools, tablets and ornaments. At Harappa the use of the script largely ceased around 1900 BCE as inscribed seals went out of use, though it may have lasted longer in other regions, for example at Rangpur in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The signs are largely pictorial, depicting objects of the Harappan world and of nature, though many abstract signs have also been identified. Some are compounds of simpler signs, and some resemble tally marks and are often interpreted as numerals. The number of principal signs is over 400, which is considered too many for each to stand for a single sound, so the script is generally believed to be logo-syllabic. Iravatham Mahadevan listed 419 distinct signs in the 1970s, while in 2015 Bryan Wells estimated around 694. Just 67 signs account for 80 per cent of usage.</p><p>Most scholars agree that the script was generally read from right to left, though exceptions are known. The direction has been deduced from external evidence, such as signs crowded on the left side as if the writer were running out of space at the end of a row. Seals were small, mostly 2–3 centimetres on a side, and animals such as bulls and elephants often accompany the text.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The first publication of a seal with Harappan symbols dates to 1875, in a drawing by Alexander Cunningham. By 1992 about 4,000 inscribed objects had been found, some as far away as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. The script has not been deciphered. The inscriptions are very short, averaging around five signs, no bilingual text like the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosetta-stone">Rosetta Stone</a> has been found, and the language is unknown. Over 100 mutually exclusive attempts at decipherment have been published since the 1920s, without scholarly consensus. Some scholars, among them Asko Parpola and Iravatham Mahadevan, argue that the language was Dravidian, while others propose an Indo-Aryan or Munda language. Steve Farmer, Richard Sproat and Michael Witzel argued in 2004 that the signs were nonlinguistic, a view Parpola disputed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Indus script” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Indus script</b> is known from some 4,000 to 5,000 inscribed objects: seals and their impressions, small tablets of clay, faience and copper, pottery, tools and ornaments.</blockquote><a href="/culture/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>BMAC objects have been found in the Indus Valley Civilisation, on the Iranian Plateau and in the Persian Gulf, and Oxus sites hold foreign goods in turn: at Gonur Depe an Elamite-type cylinder seal and a Harappan seal stamped with an elephant and <b>Indus script</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/oxus-civilisation">Oxus civilisation · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1960 B. B. Lal found that 89% of the symbols he surveyed had counterparts in the <b>Indus script</b>; Iravatham Mahadevan linked them to it, while K. Rajan sees them as the beginning of the later Brahmi script.</blockquote><a href="/culture/south-indian-megalithic-culture">South Indian megalithic culture · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li></ul></section>
